98 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbc68d1a-70a3-55d6-bb6c-c4679a0bc7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122929 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122929 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122929 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e984b7c2-1a1e-57d4-9617-f7cf0dd38393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122954 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7995f374-71ba-5524-874d-30fb981c0dab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122955 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122955 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122955 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "84cb77bd-5e51-53d1-9eeb-351dc6b50a57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122956 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122956 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122956 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "963d566f-c6d2-5d0f-972c-85bdb8d8ccd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122957 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122957 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122957 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9cc55209-b33f-5ad3-97d8-2a316c4f19c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122958 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122958 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122958 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a07be0b7-790b-5f24-8c7a-b9494a09b936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122959 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122959 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122959 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "46f843e2-8957-5597-8b4e-372f176f6e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124344 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124344 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/05/055da79921f7640b8f396653160a3ec34c4959ee4157806c0286152fa781c3aa.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1},$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ae53015-78d7-521e-8e53-edf656a0a906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124344 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $D=P^{-1}AP$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_3=-2 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-9.$\n\nThe second column of $P$ corresponds to $\\lambda=-2.$ Computing $A-\\lambda I,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -8\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1 = -x_2+2x_3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](images/c6/c6dbdd2b14d7b81925ed2d7a10441061a5aaed814d9a295dd097597431985f0b.png)\n\nComparing this with the second column of $P,$ we deduce that $x_2=0.$ Now, setting $x_3=1$ and substituting our values for $x_2$ and $x_3$ into the general solution, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{2}{1}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124360 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124360 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/99/99b941869c761efa0441ec83c5aec729d31b3f8294ec09e1ad5daa828f707d50.png)\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9610299d-5fe4-5fc2-abbc-c7dcadd5164e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124360 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-0) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{1}$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) = 1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 4.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-4I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda_2 = 4.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) + \\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 1+2 = 3. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n![](images/51/513efa38c61e97f8b8b2a777488deb28ec551de06567f8c97fa6e59527bcd8e7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124410 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124410 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bc/bc31c5e4a0e362c51b23718aa121b064b72cc6b7dd9965f6d67f7ce83776c440.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1},$ what is the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6cc93bd0-c52c-56cf-bac4-ffcd67151dce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124410 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A=PDP^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_3=0.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2+3x_3.$ Hence, the general solution is\n![](images/8e/8e827da643179ea7b1943c875f936de99bbc9a3a01ba35fcb8fb2095e2beac80.png)\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/6a/6ad3b3b6b8db1e15fb26a6eec89d02c886ec91a2c7bf675980d8a8f638805cb3.png) and ![](images/86/86e7ef62c868c3456422c0d6c5e4dcfdfafba566b1c76af2a92a90ec71ca1147.png)\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/38/38ddc9b0ce63866264f198b968f61ae7ef60174510c0badbbcc35019ecb42d0a.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n![](images/22/2258c5f9447a21fdc3d9d913aab67bb6f725bc67abdec8076d0ca0d9825232d9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124535 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124535 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124535 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124535 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124535 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175