| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )+36) & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{1}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-4)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{3}+(\\frac{4}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321426 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ is not diagonalizable" #edb/ref 175921860454